 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "me-telo-percussi",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "me telo meo percussi",
   "trans": "I struck myself with my own weapon",
   "body": "The god of love confesses he is a victim of love: <em>me telo meo percussi</em> — \"I struck myself with my own dart\". The famous archer (<em>praeclarus sagittarius</em>) has, ironically, shot himself; the emphatic <em>me ... meo</em> drives the point home.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The irony of <em>me telo meo percussi</em> is delicious: the great archer Cupid has wounded himself with his own arrow, the juxtaposed <em>me ... meo</em> stressing that the god of love has fallen in love against his own interest.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "bestia-caput",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Tragic Irony",
   "quote": "bestia ... viderer et ferro caput excideres meum",
   "trans": "I might seem a beast, and you might cut off my head with a knife",
   "body": "Cupid spells out what the sisters’ plot would have meant: he would \"seem a <em>bestia</em>\" and Psyche would cut off the very head whose eyes she loves. The reader recalls Chapter 22, where she really did raise the blade — disaster only just averted.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The tragic irony of <em>bestia ... ferro caput excideres meum</em> recalls Psyche’s actual attempt: she nearly beheaded the ‘beast’ who was her divine husband, making Cupid’s reproach cut close to the bone.\"",
   "line": ""
